<!--
  FROZEN INTERFACE - v1. Do not edit to make a test pass.

  This is the contract the unprivileged daemon serves on the SESSION bus, and
  it is the second of two seams in this project. The first
  (docs/dbus-interface-v1.xml) is between the daemon and the root helper; this
  one is between the GUI/CLI and the daemon.

    bus name     com.goblinmode.Pro.Daemon
    object path  /com/goblinmode/Pro/Daemon
    interface    com.goblinmode.Pro.Daemon

  Freezing it does NOT mean the daemon is being rewritten. It means the GUI and
  the daemon can be worked on independently, that a change to what the GUI is
  allowed to assume has to be deliberate, and that any future implementation
  has something exact to be measured against. The helper port showed what this
  buys: the freeze and its conformance suite found a real bug in the Python
  helper before a line of Rust existed.

  Everything here is unprivileged. The daemon runs as the user; anything
  needing root goes on to the helper over the other interface, which is
  authorized separately. Nothing on this interface is authorized at all, so
  every method here is reachable by anything in the user's session - that is
  the correct trust model for a per-user service, and it is why the privileged
  operations are NOT here.

  Payloads are JSON strings rather than D-Bus structs, deliberately: the shapes
  change often, and a struct signature change is a breaking interface change
  where a JSON field addition is not. The signature freeze therefore protects
  the CALL surface; the payload shapes are covered by the daemon's own tests.

  The three properties are read-only identity, matching the helper's. Nothing
  may branch on Implementation - it is for bug reports.

  ADDING a method is the only change to this file that does not need a v2:
  no existing caller breaks. It still has to be deliberate, landed in one
  commit with the implementation and this file together, and said out loud in
  the commit message. UnignoreGame was added that way - the conformance suite
  found that IgnoreGame had no inverse anywhere in the project.

  The file is stored in the canonical form produced by tests/_dbusxml.py, and
  tests/test_daemon_interface_freeze.py asserts the daemon still serves exactly
  this. If that test fails, the interface changed: either revert the change or
  cut a v2 and update every caller. Regenerating this file to match new
  behaviour is how the check stops protecting anything.
-->
